 \begin{abstract}
-This paper presents the Object-Driven Cellular Automata (ODCA) framework, a traffic microsimulation paradigm that combines the spatial simplicity of cellular automata with discrete event simulation. Each vehicle is an asynchronous process that acquires cells through a request--wait--seize--delay--release protocol, so congestion emerges from resource contention rather than from explicit gap rules, and the delayed release produces headways consistent with Newell's car-following model, yielding a triangular fundamental diagram without calibration. Drivers re-evaluate speed and direction when neighbours move rather than at fixed intervals, so computational effort concentrates where interactions occur. We derive closed-form expressions linking the protocol parameters to capacity, critical density and backward wave speed, and extend them to mixed autonomous and human-driven traffic. In 20 replications on a four-lane freeway offered 7{,}000\,veh/h, raising the autonomous share from 0\% to 70\% increases served throughput by 18.6\% (from $5{,}891 \pm 18$ to $6{,}984 \pm 35$\,veh/h, 95\% CI) and cuts average delay by 96\% (from $215 \pm 5$ to $8.7 \pm 0.2$\,s). Throughput understates the capacity effect once the network serves 99.8\% of the demand offered, after which the benefit appears as delay reduction, at a lane-drop bottleneck as well.
+This paper presents the Object-Driven Cellular Automata (ODCA) framework, a traffic microsimulation paradigm that combines the spatial simplicity of cellular automata with discrete event simulation. Each vehicle is an asynchronous process that acquires cells through a request--wait--seize--delay--release protocol, so congestion emerges from resource contention rather than from explicit gap rules, and the delayed release produces headways consistent with Newell's car-following model, yielding a triangular fundamental diagram without calibration. Drivers re-evaluate speed and direction when neighbours move rather than at fixed intervals, so computational effort concentrates where interactions occur. We derive closed-form expressions linking the protocol parameters to capacity, critical density and backward wave speed, and extend them to mixed autonomous and human-driven traffic. In 20 replications on a four-lane freeway offered 7{,}000\,veh/h, raising the autonomous share from 0\% to 70\% increases served throughput by 18.7\% (from $5{,}885 \pm 24$ to $6{,}983 \pm 35$\,veh/h, 95\% CI) and cuts average delay by 96\% (from $215 \pm 5$ to $8.6 \pm 0.3$\,s). Throughput understates the capacity effect once the network serves 99.8\% of the demand offered, after which the benefit appears as delay reduction, at a lane-drop bottleneck as well.
 \end{abstract}
 
