 The simulation results confirm the expected trends from the analytical capacity predictions:
 
-\paragraph{Throughput.} Network throughput increases with AV penetration, from $5{,}891 \pm 18$\,veh/h at $\alpha = 0\%$ to $6{,}984 \pm 35$\,veh/h at $\alpha = 70\%$, an 18.6\% improvement, and the confidence intervals are tight (coefficient of variation 0.6--1.1\%), so 20 replications resolve the differences comfortably. Most of the gain is already realised at 30\% AV ($6{,}687 \pm 27$, that is 13.5\%), and the last two scenarios are 0.1\% apart. The reason is that the network runs out of demand before it runs out of capacity: mainline and ramps offer 7{,}000\,veh/h, so $6{,}984$\,veh/h at $\alpha = 70\%$ is 99.8\% of everything that asks to enter. The all-HDV baseline, by contrast, serves 84.2\% of the offered demand and holds the rest back in the origin queues. The last three rows of Table~\ref{tab:results_mixed} show that queue directly: an S1 vehicle waits $88.1 \pm 8.5$\,s at its origin and $650 \pm 40$ vehicles per run never get on, against $0.6$\,s and one vehicle at 70\% AV. Mean cells held rises from 4.2 in S1 to 5.4 in S3 and falls back to 4.9 in S4: the faster the stream, the more cells a vehicle's delayed releases lock behind it, until the AV's shorter $\tau$ takes them back. The measured improvement is therefore a lower bound on the capacity effect: the theoretical 71\% capacity increase (Table~\ref{tab:theoretical_capacity}) cannot show up in a throughput figure that the demand caps. Where the extra capacity does show up is in delay, which keeps falling after throughput has saturated. Figure~\ref{fig:throughput_bar} visualizes the throughput and delay trends with 95\% confidence intervals.
